    <!-- ============ MODULE 1 ============ -->
    <section id="module-1" class="section module-block" data-locked="false">
      <div class="module-header">
        <span class="module-num">01</span>
        <div><h2 class="section-title" style="margin-bottom:2px;">Foundations of Responsive Design</h2><span class="diff-badge">Foundational</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="module-body">
        <p class="lede">Before media queries, before Grid — responsive design starts with a mindset shift: stop designing for a screen size, and start designing for a range.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;margin:22px 0 8px;">Why fixed layouts break</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--ink-soft);line-height:1.7;font-size:0.92rem;">In the early 2000s, most sites were built at a fixed width — usually 960px — because most monitors were roughly the same size. That assumption collapsed with the iPhone in 2007 and the explosion of tablets, phablets, and now foldables. A 960px-wide layout on a 375px phone doesn't "shrink to fit" — it gets clipped, forcing horizontal scrolling, or the browser scales the whole page down until the text is unreadable. Responsive design, a term coined by Ethan Marcotte in 2010, replaced "design for a screen" with "design for a system" — one layout, built from flexible parts, that reorganizes itself across the full range of possible viewports.</p>

        <div class="callout analogy">
          <span class="callout-label">Analogy</span>
          <p>A fixed layout is an ice cube: a rigid shape that only fits the one glass it was frozen in. A responsive layout is water: it's still fundamentally the same substance, but it takes the shape of whatever container it's poured into — a cup, a bowl, a bottle — without you having to sculpt a new one each time.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;margin:22px 0 8px;">The three pillars</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--ink-soft);line-height:1.7;font-size:0.92rem;">Marcotte's original definition still holds up, and everything in this tutorial is one of these three things:</p>
        <ul class="tidy">
          <li><b style="color:var(--ink);">Fluid grids</b> — layout widths defined in relative units (%, fr) instead of fixed pixels, so containers resize with their parent.</li>
          <li><b style="color:var(--ink);">Flexible media</b> — images and video that scale down with their container (<code>max-width:100%</code>) instead of overflowing it.</li>
          <li><b style="color:var(--ink);">Media queries</b> — CSS rules that apply only within certain viewport conditions, for the moments fluidity alone isn't enough (covered fully in Module 2).</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="diagram-wrap">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 640 210" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="The same content reflowing across desktop, tablet and mobile widths">
            <text x="60" y="20" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-faint)" font-size="10" font-family="ui-monospace,monospace">DESKTOP</text>
            <rect x="10" y="30" width="180" height="150" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--rule)"/>
            <rect x="18" y="38" width="164" height="16" rx="3" fill="var(--teal)" opacity="0.7"/>
            <rect x="18" y="62" width="50" height="110" rx="3" fill="var(--bg-panel-2)" stroke="var(--rule)"/>
            <rect x="74" y="62" width="50" height="110" rx="3" fill="var(--bg-panel-2)" stroke="var(--rule)"/>
            <rect x="130" y="62" width="52" height="110" rx="3" fill="var(--bg-panel-2)" stroke="var(--rule)"/>

            <text x="320" y="20" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-faint)" font-size="10" font-family="ui-monospace,monospace">TABLET</text>
            <rect x="250" y="30" width="140" height="150" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--rule)"/>
            <rect x="258" y="38" width="124" height="16" rx="3" fill="var(--teal)" opacity="0.7"/>
            <rect x="258" y="62" width="59" height="52" rx="3" fill="var(--bg-panel-2)" stroke="var(--rule)"/>
            <rect x="323" y="62" width="59" height="52" rx="3" fill="var(--bg-panel-2)" stroke="var(--rule)"/>
            <rect x="258" y="120" width="124" height="52" rx="3" fill="var(--bg-panel-2)" stroke="var(--rule)"/>

            <text x="540" y="20" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-faint)" font-size="10" font-family="ui-monospace,monospace">MOBILE</text>
            <rect x="500" y="30" width="80" height="150" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--rule)"/>
            <rect x="507" y="38" width="66" height="14" rx="3" fill="var(--amber)" opacity="0.8"/>
            <rect x="507" y="58" width="66" height="34" rx="3" fill="var(--bg-panel-2)" stroke="var(--rule)"/>
            <rect x="507" y="96" width="66" height="34" rx="3" fill="var(--bg-panel-2)" stroke="var(--rule)"/>
            <rect x="507" y="134" width="66" height="34" rx="3" fill="var(--bg-panel-2)" stroke="var(--rule)"/>
          </svg>
          <p class="diagram-caption">Same three content blocks, same HTML — the layout reorganizes as space shrinks: 3 columns → 2 columns → stacked.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;margin:22px 0 8px;">The units that make fluidity possible</h3>
        <table class="compare">
          <thead><tr><th>Unit</th><th>Relative to</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>px</td><td>Nothing — absolute</td><td>Borders, shadows, fine details that shouldn't scale</td></tr>
            <tr><td>%</td><td>The parent element's size</td><td>Fluid widths for grids, images, containers</td></tr>
            <tr><td>em</td><td>The current element's own font size</td><td>Padding/spacing that should scale with local text size</td></tr>
            <tr><td>rem</td><td>The root (<code>&lt;html&gt;</code>) font size</td><td>Consistent, predictable global spacing and type scales</td></tr>
            <tr><td>vw / vh</td><td>1% of the viewport width / height</td><td>Full-bleed sections, fluid type, hero heights</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;margin:22px 0 8px;">Fixed vs. fluid, live</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--ink-soft);font-size:0.9rem;">Drag the ruler below. The red box has a hard-coded <code>width:230px</code>. The green box uses <code>width:100%</code>. Watch what happens as the frame gets narrower than 230px.</p>
        <div class="ruler-widget" id="m1Ruler"></div>

        <div class="exercise-box">
          <span class="callout-label">Practical exercise</span>
          <p style="color:var(--ink-soft);font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 8px;">Open any project you have (or a blank file) and find a hard-coded pixel width on a container or image. Replace it with a percentage or <code>max-width</code>, then resize your actual browser window. Notice the exact width where it starts to look awkward — you'll use that number as a real breakpoint in Module 2.</p>
        </div>

        <ul class="tidy misconceptions">
          <li><b>"Responsive" means "mobile-friendly."</b> Mobile-friendliness is one outcome. Responsive design is the general technique of adapting to <em>any</em> size, including ultra-wide monitors and split-screen windows.</li>
          <li><b>Percentages alone solve everything.</b> Fluid units handle graceful shrinking, but at extreme widths (very narrow or very wide) you usually still need media queries to change the actual layout structure, not just resize it.</li>
          <li><b>Fluid and responsive are the same thing.</b> Fluid is one ingredient (flexible units). Responsive is the whole recipe — fluid grids <em>plus</em> flexible media <em>plus</em> media queries, working together.</li>
        </ul>

        <span class="callout-label" style="margin-top:18px;display:block;">Key takeaways</span>
        <ul class="tidy takeaways">
          <li>Fixed pixel layouts assume one screen size and break outside it — responsive design assumes a range.</li>
          <li>The three pillars are fluid grids, flexible media, and media queries.</li>
          <li>Use <code>%</code> and <code>max-width</code> for fluid sizing; reserve <code>px</code> for details that genuinely shouldn't scale.</li>
          <li><code>rem</code> gives predictable spacing; <code>vw</code>/<code>vh</code> tie sizing directly to the viewport.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
