    <!-- ============ MODULE 2 ============ -->
    <section id="module-2" class="section module-block" data-locked="true">
      <div class="module-header">
        <span class="module-num">02</span>
        <div><h2 class="section-title" style="margin-bottom:2px;">Media Queries &amp; Breakpoints</h2><span class="diff-badge">Building</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="module-body">
        <p class="lede">Fluid units get you most of the way, but at some point a layout needs to fundamentally restructure — a 3-column grid can't gracefully shrink into a usable single column just by resizing. That's what media queries are for.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;margin:22px 0 8px;">The anatomy of a media query</h3>
        <pre class="code-block"><button class="copy-btn" data-copy="mq1">Copy</button><code id="mq1">@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .layout {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
  }
}</code></pre>
        <p style="color:var(--ink-soft);font-size:0.92rem;line-height:1.7;">This rule means: "apply these styles only when the viewport is at least 768px wide." Below 768px, the browser ignores the block entirely and falls back to whatever CSS is defined outside it. That fallback is the key to mobile-first design.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;margin:22px 0 8px;">Mobile-first: min-width vs. max-width</h3>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <span class="callout-label" style="color:var(--teal);">Mobile-first (recommended)</span>
            <pre class="code-block"><code>/* base styles = small screens */
.card { width: 100%; }

@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .card { width: 50%; }
}
@media (min-width: 1200px) {
  .card { width: 33.33%; }
}</code></pre>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="callout-label" style="color:var(--amber);">Desktop-first (legacy)</span>
            <pre class="code-block"><code>/* base styles = large screens */
.card { width: 33.33%; }

@media (max-width: 1199px) {
  .card { width: 50%; }
}
@media (max-width: 767px) {
  .card { width: 100%; }
}</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p style="color:var(--ink-soft);font-size:0.9rem;">Both produce the same visual result. Mobile-first is preferred because your base (unqueried) CSS ships to every device, and small-screen devices — historically the most bandwidth- and CPU-constrained — never have to download or override desktop rules they don't need.</p>

        <div class="callout analogy">
          <span class="callout-label">Analogy</span>
          <p>Breakpoints are like clothing sizes, not tailored suits. "Medium" doesn't mean exactly 40 inches — it means a comfortable range. Good breakpoints work the same way: they're not chosen to match one specific phone model, they're chosen so the layout looks good across a <em>range</em> of widths until it needs the next size up.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;margin:22px 0 8px;">Try it: the breakpoint matcher</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--ink-soft);font-size:0.9rem;">This frame uses real CSS container queries, so it's genuinely responding to width — not a JS simulation. Drag and watch the active rule change.</p>
        <div class="ruler-widget" id="m2Ruler"></div>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;margin:22px 0 8px;">Beyond width: other useful media features</h3>
        <table class="compare">
          <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detects</th><th>Example use</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>orientation</td><td>Portrait vs. landscape</td><td>Reflow a video player when a phone rotates</td></tr>
            <tr><td>prefers-color-scheme</td><td>OS-level dark/light preference</td><td>Auto dark mode without a manual toggle</td></tr>
            <tr><td>prefers-reduced-motion</td><td>User requested less animation</td><td>Disable parallax/auto-play for accessibility</td></tr>
            <tr><td>hover / pointer</td><td>Whether the input can hover, and its precision</td><td>Skip hover-only menus on touchscreens</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="exercise-box">
          <span class="callout-label">Practical exercise</span>
          <p style="color:var(--ink-soft);font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.6;margin:0;">Take the fixed-width box from Module 1's exercise and wrap its container in a mobile-first media query: base styles stack it full-width, then at <code>min-width: 700px</code> place it beside a sibling using Flexbox. Resize your real browser window across that boundary to confirm the switch.</p>
        </div>

        <ul class="tidy misconceptions">
          <li><b>Breakpoints must match real device widths.</b> Devices number in the thousands and change yearly. Choose breakpoints where <em>your content</em> breaks, not where a specific phone's screen ends.</li>
          <li><b>You need max-width to build mobile-first.</b> Mobile-first uses <code>min-width</code> almost exclusively — your unqueried base styles already are the "mobile" version.</li>
          <li><b>More breakpoints = better design.</b> Every extra breakpoint is more CSS to maintain. Most real layouts need 2–4 well-chosen ones, not a dozen.</li>
        </ul>

        <span class="callout-label" style="margin-top:18px;display:block;">Key takeaways</span>
        <ul class="tidy takeaways">
          <li>A media query's declarations only apply when its condition is true; otherwise your base CSS stands.</li>
          <li>Mobile-first uses <code>min-width</code> and layers complexity upward as space increases.</li>
          <li>Choose breakpoints from where your actual content breaks, not from a list of device widths.</li>
          <li>Media queries can detect more than width — orientation, color scheme, motion preference, and pointer type all matter for real users.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="lock-overlay"><span class="lock-icon">🔒</span><p>Complete Module 1's quiz to unlock this module.</p></div>

      <div class="quiz" id="quiz-2">
        <h3>Module 2 check</h3>
        <p class="quiz-sub">4 questions · answer all, then check your results</p>
        <div class="quiz-body"></div>
        <div class="quiz-actions"><button class="primary quiz-check-btn" disabled>Check answers</button></div>
