    <!-- ============ MODULE 3 ============ -->
    <section id="module-3" class="section module-block" data-locked="true">
      <div class="module-header">
        <span class="module-num">03</span>
        <div><h2 class="section-title" style="margin-bottom:2px;">Responsive Layout Systems</h2><span class="diff-badge">Applying</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="module-body">
        <p class="lede">Flexbox and Grid do most of the reflow work for you — often without a single media query — because they respond to the space they're actually given, not just the viewport.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;margin:22px 0 8px;">Flexbox: one dimension, flexible</h3>
        <pre class="code-block"><button class="copy-btn" data-copy="fx1">Copy</button><code id="fx1">.row {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 1rem;
}
.row > * {
  flex: 1 1 220px; /* grow, shrink, base width */
}</code></pre>
        <p style="color:var(--ink-soft);font-size:0.92rem;line-height:1.7;"><code>flex-wrap: wrap</code> lets items drop to a new line instead of squeezing forever. <code>flex: 1 1 220px</code> tells each item "try to be 220px, but grow to fill extra space, and shrink if the row gets tight." No media query needed — the row reflows purely from available space.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;margin:22px 0 8px;">Grid: two dimensions, self-adjusting</h3>
        <pre class="code-block"><button class="copy-btn" data-copy="gr1">Copy</button><code id="gr1">.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(180px, 1fr));
  gap: 1rem;
}</code></pre>
        <p style="color:var(--ink-soft);font-size:0.92rem;line-height:1.7;"><code>auto-fit</code> tells the grid to fit as many <code>minmax(180px, 1fr)</code> columns as will comfortably reach at least 180px, then stretch them evenly to fill the rest. Shrink the container and columns drop away automatically — this single line often replaces three separate media queries.</p>

        <div class="callout analogy">
          <span class="callout-label">Analogy</span>
          <p>Flexbox is a line of people linking arms in a hallway — they squeeze or spread along one direction to fit the space. Grid is a city planner laying out blocks on a map — it thinks in rows <em>and</em> columns at once, deciding how many blocks fit before drawing a single street.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;margin:22px 0 8px;">Try it: auto-fit grid, live</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--ink-soft);font-size:0.9rem;">This grid uses the exact CSS above. No JavaScript decides the column count — the browser's layout engine does, based on the frame's actual width.</p>
        <div class="ruler-widget" id="m3Ruler"></div>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;margin:22px 0 8px;">Fluid type and responsive images</h3>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <span class="callout-label">Fluid typography</span>
            <pre class="code-block"><code>h1 {
  font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw + 1rem, 3.5rem);
}</code></pre>
            <p style="color:var(--ink-soft);font-size:0.87rem;">Reads as: never smaller than 1.75rem, never larger than 3.5rem, and scales smoothly with viewport width in between. One line replaces several font-size media queries.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="callout-label">Responsive images</span>
            <pre class="code-block"><code>&lt;img
  src="photo-800.jpg"
  srcset="photo-400.jpg 400w,
          photo-800.jpg 800w,
          photo-1600.jpg 1600w"
  sizes="(min-width: 768px) 50vw, 100vw"
  alt="..."&gt;</code></pre>
            <p style="color:var(--ink-soft);font-size:0.87rem;"><code>sizes</code> tells the browser how much viewport space the image will occupy, so it can pick the smallest file from <code>srcset</code> that still looks sharp — saving real bandwidth on phones.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <table class="compare">
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>Flexbox</th><th>Grid</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Dimensions</td><td>One at a time (row or column)</td><td>Rows and columns together</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Nav bars, button groups, form rows, content that flows</td><td>Page layout, card grids, anything with real rows/columns</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sizing driven by</td><td>Content size, unless you constrain it</td><td>Explicit tracks you define upfront</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="exercise-box">
          <span class="callout-label">Practical exercise</span>
          <p style="color:var(--ink-soft);font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.6;margin:0;">Take a list of 6 cards in your own project. Give the parent <code>display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr)); gap:1rem;</code> and delete any width media queries you had for that grid. Resize the window — confirm it reflows from one column to several with zero <code>@media</code> rules.</p>
        </div>

        <ul class="tidy misconceptions">
          <li><b>Grid replaces Flexbox.</b> They solve different problems and are commonly used together — Grid for the page skeleton, Flexbox inside individual components.</li>
          <li><b><code>auto-fit</code> and <code>auto-fill</code> are the same.</b> <code>auto-fit</code> collapses empty tracks to zero width, letting existing items stretch to fill the row; <code>auto-fill</code> keeps empty tracks reserved, which can leave visible gaps.</li>
          <li><b><code>clamp()</code> is just for font-size.</b> It works on any length value — padding, width, gaps — anywhere you want a fluid value with safe min/max bounds.</li>
        </ul>

        <span class="callout-label" style="margin-top:18px;display:block;">Key takeaways</span>
        <ul class="tidy takeaways">
          <li>Flexbox handles one-dimensional flow; Grid handles two-dimensional layout — use both, for different jobs.</li>
          <li><code>repeat(auto-fit, minmax(...))</code> creates self-adjusting column counts without media queries.</li>
          <li><code>clamp(min, preferred, max)</code> gives fluid sizing with guardrails, for type, spacing, and more.</li>
          <li><code>srcset</code>/<code>sizes</code> let the browser — not your CSS — choose the right image file per device.</li>
        </ul>
